    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "uh ok... that's a weird way to put it. can we stick to the actual numbers though? i need to know the standard psf (pounds per square foot) values usually used for residential deck loads in bc so i can actually do the math for the beam sizing.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly, let's get down to the nitty-gritty of the numbers, no goblins this time. In British Columbia, for residential decks, the standard live load is typically calculated at 40 pounds per square foot (psf). This accounts for the weight of people, furniture, and other temporary loads.\n\nThe dead load, which includes the weight of the deck structure itself, is usually calculated at 15 psf. This includes the weight of the cedar decking, the beams, posts, and any permanent fixtures.\n\nTo calculate the total load on your beams and posts, you'll add the dead load and the live load together. So, for your cedar deck, you would use a total load of 55 psf (40 psf live load + 15 psf dead load).\n\nWhen it comes to beam and post spacing, you'll want to refer to the specific building codes in British Columbia for the exact requirements, as they can vary based on the design and the materials used. However, as a general rule, beams are typically spaced between 4 to 6 feet apart, and posts are spaced between 6 to 8 feet apart. Always double-check with the local building codes to ensure compliance.",
